    'nuances' => array(
        'CAREERS copy to a candidate takes unstressed je/jouw: “so that you can reach your full potential” = “zodat je je volledige potentieel kunt benutten”; stressed jij only for contrast or a direct question (“Ben jij klaar voor de uitdaging?”).',
        'BUTTONS: object first, infinitive last (“Brochure downloaden”, “Vacatures bekijken”, “Alle filters wissen”), never an imperative or a noun replacing the verb. Utility commands: Delen, Sluiten, Verzenden, Alles uitklappen.',
        'RUNNING COPY to the reader (lead, meta, card text, chatbot) takes the plain imperative and keeps the CTA’s speech act: “Discover the … brochure” = “Ontdek de brochure …”, “Ask how …” = “Vraag ons hoe …”. A lowercase string continuing the previous one keeps its construction (“or select from the forms below” = “of een van de onderstaande formulieren kiezen”).',
        'PLACEHOLDERS: one that speaks to the reader is a full u-sentence (“Beschrijf wat u zoekt …”); a search field is object plus infinitive (“Producten zoeken …”); a status is no command (“Laden …”).',
        'KEEP THE WE-PERSPECTIVE: “At CIMAT, we offer” = “Bij CIMAT bieden we”, never third person. With a company subject and English “our”, front the our-phrase: “Met onze assemblagediensten biedt XL Machine nog meer waarde”.',
        'FIDELITY: never add, drop, strengthen or weaken a claim, modifier, number or qualifier: “the perfect solution” = “de perfecte oplossing”, never “geschikt”; a comparison keeps its object (“dan vergelijkbare machines op de markt”); no added intensifier (echt, bewezen) or regulated claim (gecertificeerd).',
        'CLAIM STRENGTH: “a leading provider” = “een toonaangevende leverancier”, never “de marktleider”; ensures = “zorgt voor / zorgt ervoor dat”; garandeert only for guarantees; “can significantly impact X” = “kan een aanzienlijke invloed hebben op X”, never “verbetert”.',
        'SCOPE WORDS: end-to-end = integraal, never uitgebreid; seamless process = vlot, integration = naadloos. global on a network is attributive (“het wereldwijde servicenetwerk”), on people postposed (“onze klanten wereldwijd”).',
        'INCLUDING: an appended open list takes “, waaronder”; zoals only right after a plural category noun (“protocollen zoals UDS”); a counted part of a total takes waarvan: “58 press releases including 12 appointments” = “58 persberichten, waarvan 12 benoemingen”.',
        'A BREAKDOWN STAYS A BREAKDOWN: “56 service locations across 18 facilities and 38 field agents” = “56 servicelocaties: 18 vestigingen en 38 servicetechnici”, never “in 18 vestigingen met …”.',
        'DESCRIPTIONS ARE NOT COMMANDS: in a service-plan, feature or spec cell an English bare infinitive becomes a noun phrase: “Perform periodic verification of machine operations” = “Periodieke controle van de machinewerking”; “Remotely monitor your machine and proactively respond …” = “Bewaking van uw machine op afstand en proactief ingrijpen …”.',
        'LIMITS: up to = tot (“tot 15.000 RPM”); down to before a lower limit = “tot op”, never bare tot: “hold tolerances down to +/- 0.002 mm” = “toleranties tot op +/- 0,002 mm aanhouden”; N+ = meer dan N (“270+ products” = “meer dan 270 producten”); +/- stays +/-, never ±; @ = bij.',
        'SIBLING SETS share one frame and number: “Balanceermachine CMT-VSR voor kernen op hoog toerental” / “Balanceermachine CMT-TR voor kernen op laag toerental”, never a compound beside a voor-phrase; sibling headings keep article and number: “Vertical/Horizontal Machine Specifications” = “Technische gegevens van de verticale/horizontale machine”. A series takes one frame: “Operator, maintenance, software or product training” = “opleiding over bediening, onderhoud, software of product”.',
        'TRANSLATE EVERY MEMBER AND BOTH HALVES: “Bauer | Aerospace” = “Bauer | Lucht- en ruimtevaart”; a heading split over lines or spans is one noun phrase: “VERTICAL / balancing machines” = “VERTICALE / balanceermachines”.',
        'FROZEN STRINGS: fix one Dutch form per product, label, CTA and FAQ question, then repeat it exactly in nav, card, H1, <title>, meta, FAQ, alt and chips. A title quoted in a sentence loses its colon: “regarding E-motor test stands: precision testing for electric motors” = “over e-motortestbanken voor precisietests van elektromotoren”.',
        'ONE TERM PER THING per page: test never alternates with proef or keuring, balanceermachine never with balancer, vestiging never with faciliteit. A repeated English word or phrase reuses its first Dutch rendering verbatim in every sentence, card and table cell (Review = Evaluatie throughout); a shortened cell keeps the full term: “Preferred (<20%)” = “Voorkeurstarief (< 20 %)”, never Voorkeur. A CTA reuses the section name: “Visit hub” = “Naar het kenniscentrum”.',
        'BRANDS NEVER TRANSLATE OR DISSOLVE: Ascential, Ascential Care, Ascentialytics, Burke Porter, Burke E. Porter, BEP Europe, Cimat, Kleinknecht, Titan, Galileo, Lismar, DATAMES, WinterPark, XL Machine, Bauer, Universal Balancing, ADAS Garage, Test & Measurement Systems, ROTORTEST, model codes. “Impossible? Done.” stays English, unchanged.',
        'BRAND BEFORE A DUTCH NOUN: in running text use van (“de e-motortestbanken van Ascential T&M”, “producten van Cimat, Galileo en XL Machine”, never “XL-machineproducten”). In a compound a one-word brand takes a readability hyphen (Titan-systeem), a multiword name one hyphen before the last part (Ascential Care-platform); never a bare space.',
        'BRAND-PLUS-DESCRIPTOR CARDS take one frame and translate the equipment class: “Titan Wet Clutch system for powertrain transmission applications” = “Titan-systeem voor natte koppelingen in transmissietoepassingen voor de aandrijflijn”; reuse it on every sibling.',
        'MODEL CODES follow the Dutch head noun, letters and case unchanged: “CMT-TR Polimer Rotor Balancing Machine” = “Balanceermachine CMT-TR Polimer voor rotoren” “Newest measuring and control unit ROTORTEST” = “Nieuwste meet- en besturingseenheid ROTORTEST”.',
        'OWNER RULE – PEOPLE’S NAMES ARE NEVER CHANGED: copy every name exactly, particle case included: “Nate Van Hekken” keeps capital Van (never “van Hekken”); nickname quotes become “…”: Zhigang “Manfred” Zhuang.',
        'JOB-TITLE POLICY: formal offices (C-level, President, Vice President, Director, General Manager) stay in full English verbatim, division after of/for included (“Vice President of Sales for Transportation”); only a region or site scope turns Dutch (“… voor Noord- en Zuid-Amerika”). Other roles are Dutch, one lowercase word: servicemanager. Never splice Dutch into an English title.',
        'APPOINTMENT HEADLINES, present tense, one pattern per verb: Hires X as Y = “neemt X in dienst als Y”; Appoints/Names = “benoemt X tot Y”; Promotes = “bevordert X tot Y”; Welcomes = “verwelkomt X als Y”; Announces X as Y = “kondigt de benoeming aan van X tot Y”; Joins … as = “treedt in dienst bij … als”; Introduces = introduceert; Launches = lanceert; Rebrands as = wordt.',
        'DE/HET: check each head noun’s gender before article, adjective ending and relative pronoun. het: systeem, platform, portaal, onderdeel, team, merk, bedrijf, gereedschap, onderhoud, toerental, koppel, debiet, werkstuk. de: machine, testbank, assemblage, kalibratie, besturing, software, oplossing. “het systeem dat”, “de machine die”; after a preposition waar-: waarmee, never “met dat”.',
        'ADJECTIVE -E after de, het, deze, dit, a possessive and in the plural; none after een or no article before a singular het-word: “een nieuw testsysteem”, “het nieuwe testsysteem”, “een nieuwe testbank”. ons only before a singular het-word (ons team, ons platform), onze elsewhere (onze klanten, onze machine).',
        'WORD ORDER: finite verb second, with inversion after any fronted element (“Daarom integreren we …”); finite verb last in subordinate and relative clauses (“…, die de productie ondersteunen.”). After “Of het nu … of …,” the main clause keeps subject-first order: “Of het nu gaat om een ontwerp vanaf nul of om optimalisatie onder druk, wij staan naast onze klanten.”',
        'SEPARABLE VERBS split in a main clause and close up at the end of subordinate clauses and te-infinitives: “Welke tests voert het systeem uit?”, “om serviceaanvragen in te dienen en te volgen”; each coordinated te-infinitive keeps its own te.',
        'NO TANGCONSTRUCTIE: no long modifier between article and noun: “Engineered for precision and versatility, these systems are …” = “Deze systemen zijn ontworpen voor precisie en veelzijdigheid en zijn …”, never “Deze voor precisie … ontworpen systemen”.',
        'HEAD-ADJACENT MODIFIERS: a relative clause or apposition stands directly after its noun, before the verb cluster: “waarmee hydrostatische aandrijvingen, die uit een pomp en een motor bestaan, worden getest”; a long apposition goes between dashes after its noun (“Onze kernwaarden – de verbintenissen die … – maken …”). Trailing participles (“…, ensuring Y”) become such a clause or a new sentence, never “zorgend voor”.',
        'PREMODIFIER CHAINS: head noun first, qualifiers after: “globally responsive, locally attentive service solutions” = “serviceoplossingen die wereldwijd snel reageren en lokaal betrokken zijn”.',
