        'POLYSEMY: the building is fabriek or bedrijf, the equipment machine or installatie: “Within your facility, where is the machine?” = “Waar bevindt de machine zich in uw fabriek?”; the company’s own sites are vestigingen; operations = activiteiten (business), bewerkingen (machining), werking (a machine).',
        'FALSE FRIENDS: business value = (meer)waarde; specific = bepaald or concreet; eventually = uiteindelijk, never eventueel; actual = werkelijk, never actueel; “where the cost of failure is high” = “waar falen hoge kosten met zich meebrengt”.',
        'ANGLICISMS: impact = invloed hebben op, never impacteren; address a problem = aanpakken, never adresseren; opportunity = kans or mogelijkheid; state-of-the-art = geavanceerd. Neutral standard: neem contact op (not contacteer ons), reserveonderdelen (not wisselstukken), mobiele telefoon (not gsm). Keep settled words: software, dashboard, cloud, e-commerce, end-of-line, engineering, retrofit, OEM, whitepaper.',
        'CALQUES: reach out = contact opnemen; powered by a technology = op basis van, by a quality = gedragen door, never aangedreven door outside mechanics; unlock performance = de prestaties naar een hoger niveau tillen; fast-paced environment = dynamische werkomgeving, never snel; robust program or package = uitgebreid (robuust only for hardware); standing on the shoulders of = voortbouwend op; off-the-shelf = kant-en-klaar; list-closing and beyond = en meer, never en verder; “to our customers’ entire satisfaction” = “dat onze klanten volledig tevreden zijn over”; world’s first X = ’s werelds eerste X, never “eerste X ter wereld”; robot-based = robotgebaseerd; “I am excited by X” = “X maakt me enthousiast”, never enthousiasmeren.',
        'THE CLIENT PROMISE has one wording sitewide: “we (will) never walk away” = “wij laten u nooit in de steek”; “we’re in this together” = “we staan er samen voor”; “lean into challenges others walk away from” = “gaan uitdagingen aan waarvoor anderen terugdeinzen”; engineer = ontwikkelen; empowered to do = in staat gesteld om, never sterker gemaakt om (“when people are empowered to tackle” = “wanneer mensen in staat worden gesteld om … aan te pakken”); only empower X with Y = X sterker maken met Y.',
        'RECRUITMENT VOCABULARY: role = functie, apply = solliciteren, req # = vacaturenummer, listing/posting = vacature (“Apply and posting links are not available for this listing.” = “Voor deze vacature zijn geen sollicitatie- of vacaturelinks beschikbaar.”), benefits = voordelen or arbeidsvoorwaarden; “Take your career to new heights” = “Til je carrière naar een hoger niveau”. “401k program” = “401k-programma”.',
        'COMPOUNDS ARE ONE WORD (no Engelse ziekte): testsysteem, klantenportaal, servicecontract, e-motortestbank. Hyphen only at a vowel clash, i.e. first part ends and second part starts with a vowel (auto-industrie, assemblage-installaties, service-ervaringen), after an abbreviation, letter or numeral (ADAS-kalibratie, CNC-frezen, 5-assig) and before a multiword name (Ascential Care-team). An English hyphenated group closes onto the Dutch noun: end-of-linetests.',
        'TUSSEN-S AND -N per Taalunie: -en only where the first part has only an -en plural (klantenportaal; but testbank, since test also has tests); -s where it is spoken (onderhoudscontract, veiligheidsnormen, gereedschapshouder); none after a verb stem (balanceermachine, slijpmachine).',
        'DIACRITICS are mandatory: industriële, geïntegreerd, efficiënt; stressed numeral één: “One division. Every solution.” = “Eén divisie. Elke oplossing.”',
        'QUOTATION MARKS: double “…”, single ‘…’ only inside a quote; never „…“, «…» or straight quotes; apostrophe ’ (casestudy’s, BEP’ers).',
        'NUMBERS: decimal comma (0,6 MPa; 34,984 mm); check the magnitude first: English “1,200 V” = 1200 V, a European “0,6” is already decimal. Four digits stay closed (1200 mm, 2500 Nm); a period groups thousands from five digits (15.000 RPM, 20.000 kg); never in years, codes or standards. Percent takes a space in this technical text (Taalunie, ISO 80000-1): 95 %, –10 %, < 10 %.',
        'OWNER RULE – UNITS AND SPECIFICATIONS ARE NEVER CHANGED: copy each unit symbol exactly as the English writes it, same letters and case: RPM stays RPM, rpm stays rpm (never tpm, omw/min, t/min); LPM (never l/min), GPM, HP (never pk), psi, MPa (never bar), bar, Nm, V, A, kW, kVA, Hz, ph (“400V 3 ph” = “400 V 3 ph”) and ″ stay; no conversion, no added equivalent. Only decimal/thousands marks and a space before the unit follow Dutch: “0,4kW” = “0,4 kW”, “400V” = “400 V”, “240v” = “240 v”.',
        'NUMBER PLUS UNIT before a noun becomes a phrase, never a compound: “16A connection” = “Aansluiting van 16 A”, “15,000 RPM Spindle” = “Spil met 15.000 RPM”.',
        'RANGES: unspaced en dash (5–25 mm, 50–60 Hz); a slash pair stays (1500/3000 RPM); dimensions take × with the source’s inch mark: “36″ x 72″” = “36″ × 72″”.',
        'SPEC TABLES: labels are bare noun phrases; spelled-out Maximum stays spelled out and agrees (Maximaal werkstukgewicht, Maximale rotordiameter), Max/Min = Max./Min.; a value puts the limit first (“6 kVA maximum” = max. 6 kVA); Not applicable = n.v.t.; empty cell –; approx. = ca.; “All 50-60Hz” = telkens 50–60 Hz; nominal/peak = “2500 Nm (nominaal) en 4000 Nm (piek)”; etc. = enz., No. = nr.',
        'DASHES AND ELLIPSIS: the spaced en dash “ – ”, never an em dash or spaced hyphen, also in Category - subtype labels and title lockups (“Horizontaal handmatig – watermeters”). … is one character; English writes it unspaced, Dutch puts a space before it after every complete word or number (“across a wide…” = “in een breed …”, “tot 20.000 kg …”), none only after a cut-off word (“wal…”); a truncated blurb stays truncated.',
        'CAPITALS: sentence case in headings, menus, tabs, filters, buttons, cards and table labels (“Industriële balanceermachines”), never English Title Case; a descriptive product name mid-sentence is lowercase; lowercase after a dash or colon unless a name follows (“CNC-frezen – afbeelding 1”); a lowercase source fragment stays lowercase.',
        'DATES: months and days lowercase; “September 22, 2026” = 22 september 2026, short 22 sep. 2026, one format per module; dateline “Shelby Township, Mich., Sept. 22, 2026 —” = “Shelby Township (Michigan), 22 september 2026 –”; 24-hour clock (“2 PM EST” = 14.00 uur EST). Exonyms: Neurenberg, Duitsland, Polen.',
        'AMPERSAND: & between Dutch words becomes en in labels, categories and headings (“Lekdetectie en koeltechniek”, “Service en ondersteuning”); keep & only inside a company, division or brand name (Test & Measurement, H. Kleinknecht & Co.).',
        'UI CHROME is translated in full, aria and counters included: a sort option is a noun (“Sorteren op: populariteit”); orientation filters stay adjectives (Verticaal, Horizontaal); manual = handmatig, automatic = automatisch, automated = geautomatiseerd; “No categories match that search.” = “Geen categorieën gevonden voor deze zoekopdracht.”; a counter control puts the infinitive last and keeps the category label’s capital: “Show 8 subcategories under Balancing Machines” = “8 subcategorieën onder Balanceermachines tonen”.',
        'KEEP THE ARTICLE that English headlines and spec labels drop before a singular count noun: “Appoints Divisional CEO” = “benoemt een CEO voor de divisie”, “Max. weight of symmetrical rotor” = “Maximaal gewicht van een symmetrische rotor”.',
